{
  "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
  "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:58 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Scott L. Nelson (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Since the inception of the Rules Enabling Act, this Court has repeatedly held that within their scope of operation, rules promulgated under that Act govern the practice and procedure of Federal courts in diversity and Federal question cases alike."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Scott L. Nelson (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I don't think that it's a substantive rule because it reflects a policy. The policy here, as described by the New York Court of Appeals in the Sperry case, is that the -- the legislature believed that class actions were not necessary in this category of cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But this is a procedural standard that has a manifestly substantive purpose, which is to restrict recoveries of penalties."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Have they applied it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0000-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Can I ask you one brief hypothetical?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Well, you can make it a claim brought under the insurance code, instead of against insurance companies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: How is it different from security for costs? I mean, that's what I started with. That's -- there's nothing in the Federal rules that say security for costs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Christopher Landau",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As some of Justice Ginsburg's initial questions point out, this case falls within the heartland of Erie because allowing plaintiffs to recover State law penalties in Federal court that they can't recover in State court on a State law cause of action would powerfully distort ex ante forum choices, which is precisely what the Erie doctrine seeks to avoid."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Christopher Landau",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0001-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "individual action, and that when you got -- when you tried to make it into a class, that that would be overenforcement of those."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Landau",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, Your Honor, if New York decides, for substantive reasons -- and we are talking about New York causes of action --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But the question is New Mexico causes of action. Can they decide that they don't want actions from outside of the State to be brought as class actions?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Nelson",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2268f733e3d0-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would like to begin with the point that my friend made about the \"unless\" clause in 901(b) and that that somehow indicated that it applied only to New York State statutes. In fact, the New York courts have applied that \"unless\" clause to Federal statutes, holding in one case that the Truth in Lending Act satisfied the \"unless\" clause because it authorized a class action, and in another that the Telephone Consumer Protection Act did not because it didn't authorize a class action."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1008",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "2268f733e3d0b10606e25cff8f86fb6f08c5964eda773abc33e5944c292c4123",
